00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:19.250
You've said before that
stress can be good for us,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.220
but how do we know
when it's too much?

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.840
That's a good question,
and I should preface that

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.660
by saying that there's
some incredible work that

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.550
was done by a colleague
of mine, Dr. Alia Crum.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.730
Ali is how she goes
by, Dr. Allie Crum.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.920
Who is a-- she was a
Division I athlete.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.750
She's a tenured professor
of psychology at Stanford.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.270
She's a licensed clinical
psychologist, like everyone.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.310
So when I look
around my colleague,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.393
I'm like, oh, my goodness.

00:02:49.393 --> 00:02:51.250
Who are these people?

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:52.600
Very humbling place to be.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.350
But she's made some
important discoveries,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.110
and I want to just
highlight one, which

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.280
is this notion of mindset.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.930
And no, these are
not placebo effects,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.508
but let me just tell you.

00:03:00.508 --> 00:03:02.800
When you think about stress,
how you think about stress

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.743
is really important.

00:03:03.743 --> 00:03:05.410
First of all, you
can't lie to yourself.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:10.240
But if for instance you watch
a one or three-minute video,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.733
as she's given subjects
in her lab about stress,

00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:15.400
and it tells you all
the terrible things

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.100
that stress does to your
immune system and sleep,

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.432
then you experience
those things-- increases

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:21.640
in blood pressure, et cetera.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.973
However, if you watch a one
to three-minute video that's

00:03:23.973 --> 00:03:26.350
also true about the fact
that stress can sharpen

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.660
your decision making for
certain kinds of things,

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.730
actually, can accentuate
your immune system.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.910
I wish someone had helped me get
this narrative right out there.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.830
Stress does not deplete
your immune system,

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.420
unless it goes on
a long, long time.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.670
We'll talk about what
long means in a moment.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:40.660
Why would it?

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.780
Think about it.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.790
If you had to fast
and move with family

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.840
or weather a storm of any
kind, emotional or physical,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.660
and you got sick, that
wouldn't make any sense.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.468
It's usually after
you're stressed.

00:03:54.468 --> 00:03:57.010
If you've ever been go, go, go,
or taking care of a loved one

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.177
or studying or working hard,
and then you finally go

00:03:59.177 --> 00:04:01.510
on vacation, you rest, you
arrive, and you get sick,

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.000
it's because your
immune system shut down.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:04.780
It stopped.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.800
Your immune system is mobilized
by that alertness side

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.898
of your autonomic nervous
system, but you do need sleep.

00:04:12.898 --> 00:04:14.440
You do need sleep,
and it's actually,

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.435
we think, the slowing
of circulation.

00:04:16.435 --> 00:04:18.310
And this is why it's
probably not a good idea

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.290
to exercise if
you're already sick,

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.710
and if you're
veering towards sick

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.530
probably limit the intensity
of any kind of interaction

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.410
as best you can and
just still yourself.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.490
Well, Ali's lab has clearly
shown us over and over again

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.950
that what we know, our
knowledge base, really

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.300
does shape the physiology
over those outcomes.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.558
Now, you know that stress
is both bad and good,

00:04:39.558 --> 00:04:40.350
so which one is it?

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.700
Does it average to nothing?

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.690
No.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.870
It turns out that
you can bias this

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.170
in one direction or the other,
depending on which information

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.760
you're listening to more often.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.250
I think this is
really important.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.310
I still am trying to
get my head and my mind

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.340
around what's happened
over the last few years

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.390
and where it's placed us.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.473
Like where did it land us?

00:05:01.473 --> 00:05:04.620
Are we more resilient now, or
are we just really beat up?

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.280
I don't know.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.220
I really don't know,
but I think how

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.810
we interpret the
last few years is

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.740
going to make a big difference
in terms of how it impacts us.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.310
How do we know if we are--

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.330
thanks, yeah.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.070
What we're missing
out there, I think,

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.320
is a narrative from somebody
that people listen to.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.153
And I'm not saying that
person should be me.

00:05:24.153 --> 00:05:27.723
In fact, it shouldn't be me but
somebody that can help us frame

00:05:27.723 --> 00:05:29.640
what's just happened to
us, much like a good--

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.700
I think the world needs a
good therapist, basically.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.010
Needs a very good therapist.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.790
So I would call
short-term stress, which

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.070
is very beneficial for us,
is the kind of stress that

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.710
happens on the order of a
day, two, or three days.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:44.580
Not a problem.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.710
Even if you feel torn
apart, provided you can get

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:47.880
rest afterwards.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.710
Long-term stress is
the kind of stress

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.830
that really starts to impede
your sleep-wake cycle,

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.465
make your dreams more stressful
and more like nightmares,

00:05:55.465 --> 00:05:56.590
and they're not going away.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:00.000
And I can promise you
that for those of you that

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.170
have challenges with
accumulation of stress

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.630
from the past that's now
compounded by what's happening

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.720
now, et cetera,
the solution does

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.200
seem to be to get yourself
into a supported environment

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.410
of some kind that will allow you
to go through a full catharsis.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.440
Again, it doesn't have
to require psychedelics.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.080
That can take you
through the full ride

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.990
of autonomic intensity--

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.600
catharsis of some sort--
and then relaxation.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.120
That does seem to be
what snaps people out

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.610
of what we would call
longer term stress

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.700
and historical stress.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.620
There's even the question
of whether or not

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.320
focusing directly on the trauma
and the story is so critical.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.127
I think it is.

00:06:42.127 --> 00:06:43.710
Obviously that's
something that should

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.370
be done with a
clinician, but stress

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.710
that impedes your sleep for
three nights or more, that

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.220
shifts the pattern of dreams
to more anxious dreams, that

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.780
is more long-term stress.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.150
And for that, you need
to take it seriously,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.990
and it's the kind of thing where
if you don't take it seriously,

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.870
it can start degrading things
like your immune system,

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:04.560
et cetera.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.050
And I would say that
under those conditions,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.630
use sleep as a good marker.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:13.410
In fact, I'm sure there are
some clinicians in the room.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.930
I mean, one of the questions
that is used as a diagnostic

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.193
for whether or not
people are depressed

00:07:18.193 --> 00:07:19.860
or anxiously depressed
is whether or not

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.330
they are sleeping well or not.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.030
Again, language is not
very good at parsing

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.330
what's going on inside.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.360
We have to look to
behaviors and regularity

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.960
of sleep-wake cycles, et cetera.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.420
Hopefully that was at
least a partial answer.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.940
I tried to be accurate,
but if I were exhaustive,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.250
I might actually
cure insomnia someday

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:45.620
with these podcasts
if nothing else.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.050
How has hypnosis been
impacting my life?

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.310
I'm in hypnosis.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.670
No, the-- I actually spent most
of the afternoon in hypnosis.

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:55.490
Before I do these, I spend
a good hour in hypnosis.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.090
Again, self-directed hypnosis--
gosh, it's so unfortunate.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:01.970
I keep talking to David
Spiegel about this.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:05.330
Again, his dad was
one of the originators

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:10.045
of hypnosis as a valid
psychiatric treatment.

00:08:10.045 --> 00:08:11.420
There's even the
idea that things

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.642
like EMDR, the scanning
of eyes back and forth

00:08:13.642 --> 00:08:15.350
while reporting a
narrative, may actually

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:18.380
be capturing some of the
elements of hypnosis.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.642
Again-- and this is
why, at least for me,

00:08:20.642 --> 00:08:22.850
I appreciate the opportunity
to come together tonight

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:23.900
to talk about principles.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:25.460
I would hope that
after tonight, you

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.610
could look at any
practice, anything,

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.800
any compound, any
breathing exercise,

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.740
and just be able
to frame up which

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.677
continuum, where
on the continuum,

00:08:35.677 --> 00:08:37.010
what's it really designed to do.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.101
Move you up towards alertness
or down towards calm.

00:08:40.101 --> 00:08:41.809
And in fact, because
I can't help myself,

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.059
I'll just tell you for
all the breathing stuff,

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:45.530
it can be made very simple.

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:47.540
If your exhales
are longer and more

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.140
vigorous than your inhales,
you're going to get calm down.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.000
If your inhales
are longer and more

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.100
vigorous than your
exhales, you're

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.760
going to become more alert.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.610
And if you hold your
breath, just eventually

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:01.270
just start breathing again.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.700
The physiology maps
perfectly to that.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.555
That maps perfectly
to the physiology.

00:09:05.555 --> 00:09:07.930
And if, for instance, you do
box breathing-- inhale hold,

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.980
exhale holding, and
this kind of thing--

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.040
well, you're going to stay
right where you're at.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:15.430
You're going to be on an
even plane more or less.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.368
So that hopefully captures all
of breathwork in one sentence.

00:09:18.368 --> 00:09:20.410
Now, I'll answer the
question you were asking me.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.290
Hypnosis, it's impact in my
life in a couple of ways.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:30.330
One way is more from a
practical scientific way, which

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.450
is that my laboratory works on
vision, and we work on stress.

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:36.255
And in some ways, those
might seem divorced.

00:09:36.255 --> 00:09:38.130
Although now with the
cuttlefish and the fact

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.122
that your eyes narrow
their field of view

00:09:40.122 --> 00:09:41.580
when you're stressed,
et cetera, it

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.290
should become
obvious why that is.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.390
But hypnosis also
takes advantage

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:49.080
of this really weird
cool feature, which is--

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.570
and it's always weird when
you do a group exercise,

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.875
but I can't really
see you all that well.

00:09:53.875 --> 00:09:56.250
I certainly can't see your
eyes well enough to know this.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.780
But David Spiegel, there's
actually something in the--

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.610
this is a valid thing called
the Spiegel Eye Roll Test,

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.830
and it's not the
teenage eye roll.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.100
That when you look up while
not moving your head-- when

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.410
you look up, you
actually are engaging

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.770
circuits in your brainstem
that are involved

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.390
in generating alertness.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.880
And when you look down
and your eyes close,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:18.780
the opposite is true.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.450
You're engaging circuits in
the brain that are taking you

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:22.680
into a calmer state.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.870
Now, wouldn't it be wonderful if
all you had to do was look down

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:26.340
and you'd be calm and look
up and you'd be alert.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.715
That doesn't quite
work that way.

00:10:27.715 --> 00:10:29.520
But to induce
hypnosis, what they do

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.210
is they have people look up,
and then while looking up,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:36.400
close their eyes, which
is actually hard to do.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.190
Some people can't do it.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:41.765
Their eyes roll forward, hence
the Spiegel Eye Roll Test.

00:10:41.765 --> 00:10:43.140
Some people, their
eyes get-- you

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.652
see the whites of their eyes,
and it looks really spooky,

00:10:45.652 --> 00:10:47.610
and they're looking up
while their eyes closed.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.430
Those people are very
prone to hypnosis.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:51.330
Why?

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:55.020
Well hypnosis is a
state of deep relaxation

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:56.700
with alertness and focus.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.400
It's a contextual
narrowing-- excuse me.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.440
So it's like being
in early stage sleep,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.650
and that's why stage hypnosis
works with telling people

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:05.770
to do certain things.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:07.650
It's not that they
don't care or they're

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.960
under the control
of the hypnotist.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.710
It's that they forget
what's around them.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:12.210
Why?

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:16.620
Because their mind is focused
internally and on the dialogue

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.728
with the hypnotist and is not
paying attention to context.

00:11:20.728 --> 00:11:22.020
So it's a narrowing of context.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.540
But hypnosis for me has
been very useful because A,

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.130
it validated the relationship
between vision and states

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:27.630
of mind.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.980
It also checks off
this box, again,

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.680
which is that to
access neuroplasticity,

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:32.388
what do you need?

00:11:32.388 --> 00:11:36.660
You need focus plus you need
a state of deep relaxation.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.010
Usually, first focus then
sleep or non-sleep deep rest.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.740
But Spiegel and his
daddy figured out

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.100
because they're way smarter than
I am that you can get people

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:50.490
into that perfect state
of neuroplasticity

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:52.320
by combining them
both in real time

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.630
through this atypical
thing we call hypnosis.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:12:00.360
So I do daily or maybe every
other day hypnosis script

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.400
that's about-- it's
self-directed hypnosis

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.870
script of about 5 to
15 minutes, usually,

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.840
trying to get myself to be
less pissed off about something

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.002
that I'm really pissed
off about, frankly.

00:12:12.002 --> 00:12:13.710
I imagine the stuff
I'm really pissed off

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.810
about in the screen
on the left side.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.310
I think about all the things
that make me feel good,

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.290
and then I keep thinking
about how angry I am.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.680
This is really how it goes.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:23.550
And then over time,
I've noticed, well,

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.530
you're coupling that
bodily state of calm

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:26.430
to the anger thing.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.160
This is all very hard
to do in talk therapy.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:30.600
No disrespect to talk therapy.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:34.020
There's a tremendous advantage
to talk therapy that I myself

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.510
have benefited from
it, although according

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.660
to certain people in
my life not enough.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.870
But hypnosis works
because it's capturing

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.790
neuroplasticity processes.

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:49.988
Thank you for that question.

00:12:49.988 --> 00:12:51.530
What are the most
effective protocols

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:52.920
for boosting the microbiome?

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:53.420
Oh.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.300
Well, here, I'm very
fortunate because my upstairs

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:00.020
neighbor at Stanford is the
great Justin Sonnenburg,

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.900
and he and his wife Erika
run this amazing lab defining

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:04.700
all the principles of
the gut microbiome,

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:06.483
and they have a
really cool idea.

00:13:06.483 --> 00:13:08.150
I don't know if he's
serious about this,

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:11.910
but I can't help
but chuckle when

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:14.080
I think that this
might actually be true.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:15.930
We all know-- this
is definitely true--

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.910
that we all carry
around trillions

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.110
of little micro-bacteria.

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:20.490
Not just in our gut--

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.020
that goes from one
end of our throat

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.020
to the other-- any
mucosal lining.

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.880
Eyes, genitalia, nose--
we have microbiomes

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.440
in our nose, et cetera.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.440
This is why we're heading
into the winter months

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.330
being a nasal breather.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:37.390
That sounds like crazy new agey
stuff-- be a nasal breather.

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.230
There's actually a book written
by Paul Ehrlich and Sandra Kahn

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.560
at Stanford with a foreword
by Jared Diamond and Robert

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:44.100
Sapolsky.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:46.110
The book, Jaws, so these
are some heavy hitters.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:49.440
And there's very good evidence
that people who mouth breathe

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.230
are making themselves
more prone to illness

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:54.280
when nasal breathing
because of the microbiome.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.290
It's just a better
filter for germs.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.610
So this winter and always really
try and be a nasal breather

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:00.190
all the time.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:02.690
One of the best ways to do that
if you're not into the mouth

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:05.700
taping thing,
which people do, is

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:08.460
to try and do some
of your exercise

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:09.872
with just nasal breathing.

00:14:09.872 --> 00:14:11.580
It'll take a little
while to get used to,

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.860
but I'm not good at the
nasal breathing thing

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.850
because I'm always talking.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:19.610
The microbiome is all
over us and in us.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:20.330
It's on our skin.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:22.038
We're actually exchanging
it when we meet

00:14:22.038 --> 00:14:22.940
and we shake hands.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:25.820
Do you know what happens usually
in the first 10, 15 seconds?

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:27.980
Data from Noam Sobel's
lab at the Weizmann

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:29.450
has shown that we wipe our eyes.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:32.060
We wipe other people's
molecules on us.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.710
We're really good at that.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.048
Just watch these interactions.

00:14:35.048 --> 00:14:37.340
Now, everyone's going be
doing the germ free handshake.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:38.910
They're going to be fist bump.

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:44.870
But there is this idea that
maybe we are the house cats.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.370
Maybe we're not
just transporting

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.590
all these microbiota
because it's good for us.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.280
What if we're just the vehicles,
and they're running the planet?

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.340
And they're like, oh, we're
running out of some stuff.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.340
Like we should figure out
somebody to take us to Mars,

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:59.850
and then we'll take over Mars.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.340
I mean, maybe it's all them.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.240
And Justin was the
one that told me that,

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.698
and I was like, that's kind of
eerie if you think about it.

00:15:05.698 --> 00:15:08.510
But there might be these
other intelligences that

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:11.060
are hijacking us,
and that's scary

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:14.600
because we like to think that
we're in charge, and who knows.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:18.110
What's good for your microbiome,
or what's good for them

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:19.400
that is?

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:23.900
Well, prebiotic fiber
seems to be very important,

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:25.970
but the studies of
fiber, at least,

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:29.480
as it relates to the microbiome
are somewhat controversial.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.670
There was a study done
on humans at Stanford

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.070
by Chris Gardner and
Justin Sonnenburg

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:37.160
that showed that people
who eat one to four--

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:38.750
you have to ramp up--

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:40.730
servings of low sugar
fermented foods.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:43.220
This would be your kimchi,
your natos, your sauerkraut,

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.190
your kiefer's, your
kombuchas, et cetera.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:51.670
Per day develop a very
robust microbiome,

00:15:51.670 --> 00:15:54.290
and fiber did not do that.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.770
In fact, fiber increased
the so-called inflammatory,

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.390
which is the markers
for inflammation,

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.880
but that doesn't mean
that fiber is bad.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:03.138
Fiber actually is--
getting enough fiber

00:16:03.138 --> 00:16:04.930
is correlated with a
number of other things

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:08.830
that are great, like reduced
cardiovascular disease,

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.480
for instance, cancers of
the colon, for instance--

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:14.560
so fiber and gut microbiota.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.260
Prebiotics, probiotics,
probably only necessary

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:20.447
if you have a dysbiosis-- if
you've been taking antibiotics,

00:16:20.447 --> 00:16:22.780
or if for some reason you're
depleted of the microbiome.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.780
One of the great ways to
deplete your microbiome

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.740
is to just eat highly
processed foods,

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.380
but hopefully most
people aren't doing that.

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:33.010
So prebiotic fiber and these
low-sugar fermented foods,

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:35.500
and then someone
always says beer.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:36.790
So and always, yes.

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:39.050
Yes, beer will support
your microbiome,

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:40.610
but it might do
other things too.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:46.700
So in general, low-alcohol,
low-sugar fermented foods

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:48.680
reduce the number of
inflammatory markers.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:51.140
That's very, very clear
from the Sonnenburg data.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:53.670
And then there are
other ways, of course.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.980
The microbiome, actually,
interacts with temperature.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.563
So the cold exposure
thing is actually

00:16:57.563 --> 00:16:59.600
good for your microbiome, but--

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.560
and I want to really
emphasize this.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.190
If you hear about studies
that such and such improves

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:06.560
such and such, keep
in mind that anything

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:10.060
that improves your
sleep, your microbiome,

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.730
or your social
interactions will improve

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:14.720
basically everything else.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.800
And those are what we call
modulating-- excuse me--

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.369
effects, not mediating effects.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:21.940
This is really important, and
we teach first year graduate

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:23.680
students and medical
students about this.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:26.347
Like for instance, if there were
a fire alarm pulled right now--

00:17:26.347 --> 00:17:28.600
God forbid-- it would modulate
all of your attention,

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:31.420
but would you say that fire
alarms mediate Attention No.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:33.730
It's not directly in
the line of mechanism,

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.230
but it can adjust an
existing mechanism.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:38.590
So great sleep is
great for everything,

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.880
but it doesn't
control it directly.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.030
And so things like
getting great sleep,

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:45.940
keeping your microbiome healthy,
getting enough sunlight,

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:48.220
et cetera-- they provide
a kind of buoyancy

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:50.350
to all the organs and
systems of your body,

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.890
but they aren't necessarily
the thing that cures ADHD.

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:57.010
But of course, if you have
ADHD or issues with focus,

00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:59.380
getting enough sleep will help.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:01.810
Is nutrition the way
to cure your ADHD?

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.510
No, but if you improve
your gut microbiome,

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:06.730
it's very likely that your
neurotransmitter systems

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:07.540
will improve.

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:10.180
Limiting sugar will help,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:16.690
So there's reason to think that
great sleep, solid nutrition,

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:19.840
microbiome, social
interactions, exercise--

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.700
those are the big five.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.860
There are others too, of course.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:27.550
With those you set
a good buoyancy

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:29.402
to all the other
systems, and then we

00:18:29.402 --> 00:18:31.360
get into the things of
how to directly increase

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.000
focus or modulate dopamine
and so on and so forth.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:37.904
Oh.

00:18:37.904 --> 00:18:39.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:43.790
Well, I realize some
people are afraid of dogs.

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.890
We actually have a dog
stimulus in our fear lab.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:47.598
We get people that
are terrified of dogs.

00:18:47.598 --> 00:18:49.340
We hired this dog
trainer guy who

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.670
has these pit bulls that will
attack you while you're in VR.

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.800
By the end, people are a
little more comfortable

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.190
with dogs in general.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.980
For you and me, if you're
not afraid of dogs,

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:03.410
that's not terribly terrifying.

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:06.980
But if you are, even
the thought of that

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:08.960
can be pretty terrifying.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:11.720
A couple of things-- people have
thought about the eye contact

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.220
thing.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.250
They make eye contact.

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:14.780
We're big on eye contact.

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:16.790
Humans too.

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:20.480
Eye contact is meaningful in
terms of oxytocin release.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:21.230
That's all real.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.047
Those data are-- the
more and more data

00:19:23.047 --> 00:19:25.130
that come out from better
studies-- eye contact is

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:25.920
a big deal.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.040
I think it's also that
just the dogs are always

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.980
game to show up exactly
where you want to meet them,

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:34.520
and they always show up in
their most loving possible state

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:35.810
for them.

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:38.330
It's a pretty simple
equation if you get it right.

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.940
They need proper care, but--

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:44.645
I mean, Costello was unique
because the bulldog also--

00:19:44.645 --> 00:19:46.520
you don't want to get
me going on dog breeds,

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:50.660
but the bulldog also looks
disappointed all the time.

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:52.165
And then you do
something it likes,

00:19:52.165 --> 00:19:54.290
and then it looks delighted,
and pretty soon you're

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:57.235
working for their approval.

00:19:57.235 --> 00:19:58.610
And we were like
the odd couple--

00:19:58.610 --> 00:19:59.300
me and him.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:01.850
And I realize he's got me
trained really, really well

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:05.030
to do my best to please
him and delight him,

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:06.270
which delighted me.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.930
And there I was on the hook.

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:09.890
So that's one reason.

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.900
I think there's also
another reason, which

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.888
is super nerdy, which
is this C tactile fiber

00:20:13.888 --> 00:20:15.680
thing, which is you
have these little nerve

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:17.280
endings in your skin.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.240
And we know, of course,
that oxytocin is

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:22.330
released from parent and child.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.080
We know this from
neuroimaging, et cetera.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:28.730
We know oxytocin is released
from non-sexual romantic touch.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:31.610
One of the things that is
very powerful for the release

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.770
of oxytocin-- very powerful--

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:38.990
is non-sexual grooming touch
among members of a species

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:40.390
or even across species.

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:44.300
So those pictures of monkeys
picking around in each other,

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.510
or people who insist on
popping things on one another,

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:50.240
or people who go to the
hairdresser or the barber

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.170
and they like touch.

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:52.670
It doesn't even
have to be massage--

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:54.710
massive oxytocin release.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:56.450
Those data don't
get as much play

00:20:56.450 --> 00:21:00.590
as all the data on
oxytocin and love.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.510
It's called the love
hormone, but it's

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.870
basically a neurochemical
signaling system

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:09.440
for this interaction, feels
good, and is very much

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:10.198
of the present.

00:21:10.198 --> 00:21:11.990
And I think that's an
important distinction

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:12.980
to make more broadly.

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:18.290
Is that dopamine is really
about the pursuit of all things

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:20.240
beyond the confines of our skin.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:21.530
I'm going to get that thing.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.000
I want that because it's
all about anticipation.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.000
And when you have some
distance between yourself

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:27.950
and the thing that you
think will deliver,

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:30.110
whatever it is you
want, usually pleasure

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:32.300
in some form or another,
or excitement, whatever

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:35.480
your pleasure is or
combined, then you actually

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:36.290
have to mobilize.

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:38.123
And dopamine is the
precursor to adrenaline.

00:21:38.123 --> 00:21:39.457
A lot of people don't know that.

00:21:39.457 --> 00:21:41.030
Adrenaline is made
from dopamine.

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.278
It gets you into motion.

00:21:42.278 --> 00:21:43.820
Then you have the
reward systems that

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:47.160
are more about what you have
from your skin surface inward.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.970
So this is gentle
touch, holding hands,

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:53.420
and indeed stroking your dog.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:54.650
Probably does that for you.

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:56.900
It activates these C
fibers, as they're called,

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:00.440
in your skin, which feed
right into these serotonin

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:01.620
and oxytocin system.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.010
So the brain-- sounds a
little pop psychology-ish,

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:07.130
but it's a real thing, and
it exists in essentially all

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:08.010
mammalian species.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.218
So I think a lot of us just
like dogs because they'll

00:22:10.218 --> 00:22:12.500
let us just pet them all day.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:15.020
Some people like to be touched
a little bit more or less,

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:17.390
even when they trust is all--

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:19.790
it's this consensual,
age appropriate, context

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:22.280
appropriate-- in this
case, species appropriate.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:25.040
Those are the conditions,
very important.

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:27.200
When I was a kid, I had--

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:27.982
I don't know why.

00:22:27.982 --> 00:22:29.190
My sister is in the audience.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.440
I don't why she decided to
do this in the first place,

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:34.507
but I loved having
my face done--

00:22:34.507 --> 00:22:35.090
pet like that.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:36.590
I still like it,
but don't try it

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.110
because she's the only one--
she can't do it anymore even.

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:41.420
So we all have these
things that feel good,

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:42.920
and I think it feels
good because it

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:44.660
releases these chemicals.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:46.490
And these are ancient systems--

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:50.365
ancient, ancient systems that
we all have, and I think dogs

00:22:50.365 --> 00:22:50.990
let us do that.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:54.230
And cats toy with
us with this one.

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:56.760
Because there are those
cats that let you pet them,

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.700
but most of the time
they're doing it to you,

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.140
and then they withdraw.

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:02.020
And I don't know many
dogs that do that.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:04.520
So I think people, I'm going
to answer the question finally.

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:06.980
I think dogs we love them
so much because they let

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:08.600
us pet them as much as we want.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:11.480
And cats play this
very diabolical game

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.330
that's a lot more like
human relationships.

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:15.104
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:20.280
How can night owls best
function in a society

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:23.010
made for morning birds?

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:25.320
Can you change your
chronotype or do us night

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:26.760
owls just have to suffer?

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:27.270
OK.

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:30.150
Well, as a former
night owl, I used

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:33.180
to work long hours in the lab.

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:35.430
I still work long hours
but less in the lab.

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:38.130
Just so happens that's
the way the career goes.

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:40.022
I'd put tinfoil on the windows.

00:23:40.022 --> 00:23:40.980
I would lock the doors.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:43.590
I'd blast the music, and I would
stay there over the holidays,

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:47.160
until I had to go home just
for the holiday events,

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:48.600
and my clock would drift.

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:51.090
So I became a night owl, and
then my clock would flip,

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:52.290
and everyone was gone.

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:54.750
Your mind gets really
tweaked when you're not

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:55.800
interacting with anybody.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:59.040
By the way, seeing faces
in the morning and seeing

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:00.660
faces at some point
during the day

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:03.480
once you're ready to face
the day, very important

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:04.680
for mental health.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:06.870
This is something I wish
more people knew about.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:08.430
It also-- and here,
I'm not trying

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:10.920
to evoke any sentimentality.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.710
But when you think about
people who just are clearly

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:17.400
not doing well, whether
or not they have shelter

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:20.040
or not-- it's how often do
we actually make direct eye

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:21.120
contact nowadays?

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.670
It's not very often.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:27.897
So eye contact is important,
but I've also shifted

00:24:27.897 --> 00:24:28.980
to being a morning person.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:32.820
So here's the thing-- if you
are a true night owl, that

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:35.848
means that your circadian clock,
meaning the genes that control

00:24:35.848 --> 00:24:37.890
the area of your brain
and your hypothalamus that

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:42.570
controls wake-sleep cycles,
is fundamentally different.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:46.000
Very unlikely you'll
become a morning person

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.990
without being a kind of
angry morning person.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:52.600
So you can use that argument,
and you can cite me.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:56.830
However, as we get
older, it is true

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:59.770
that the amount of slow
wave sleep to REM sleep

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:02.080
tends to change,
and we can do better

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:04.240
on shorter bouts
of sleep, mostly

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:07.300
because we're getting less
rapid eye movement sleep.

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.250
And even if we try, we can't.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:11.380
Those people would
probably be better off

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.230
sticking to a limited
amount of sleep at night,

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:15.550
and then getting a short nap.

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:17.920
The rule of naps is
nap if you want to.

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:20.507
Don't if you don't want,
but not if it interferes

00:25:20.507 --> 00:25:21.590
with your nighttime sleep.

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:24.090
And if you can't nap, do some
sort of non-sleep deep breaths

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:24.790
or NSDRs.

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:26.830
I refer to it as
non-sleep depressed.

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.620
So you can probably shift
your clock by anywhere

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:34.580
from two to eight hours, and
that's true for lag as well.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.230
Light is going to
be the best way,

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:37.855
but if you really
want to shift, you're

00:25:37.855 --> 00:25:39.940
going to have to stack
the big three or four.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:42.167
Light-- so get light when
you want to be awake.

00:25:42.167 --> 00:25:44.500
Temperature-- you have to
increase your body temperature

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:45.000
to wake up.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.240
You have to decrease body
temperature to go to sleep.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:49.240
Keep in mind if you get into
an ice bath or cold shower,

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.615
you get very, very
cold, but then

00:25:50.615 --> 00:25:54.680
what happens it's like putting
an ice pack on the thermostat.

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:55.930
Your body temperature goes up.

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:58.540
Remember, thermogenesis--
that's the warming of the body

00:25:58.540 --> 00:25:59.377
in response to cold.

00:25:59.377 --> 00:26:01.960
Of course, if you stay in a long
time, you'll get crispy cold.

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:03.280
You'll turn into a popsicle.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.885
But the idea is that if
you take a cold shower,

00:26:05.885 --> 00:26:08.260
and you get some bright light,
and you get some exercise,

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:10.930
and you drink some caffeine,
you can train your system

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.060
to expect that at a
certain time of day--

00:26:13.060 --> 00:26:15.803
and you'll want to go to sleep
a little bit earlier or much

00:26:15.803 --> 00:26:18.470
earlier, and you'll want to wake
up when you stack those things.

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:22.555
But that also means not taking
caffeine and cold showers

00:26:22.555 --> 00:26:23.930
and doing exercise
late at night,

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:24.890
so it's going to take some work.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:26.260
But those are the big four.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:27.370
It's going to be light.

00:26:27.370 --> 00:26:29.110
It's the most
powerful way to shift.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:30.370
More light, awake.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.020
Less light, asleep.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:33.790
Temperature increase, awake.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.190
Temperature decrease, asleep.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:37.420
Food is the other one.

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.670
Eating-- you can force
yourself to eat breakfast,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.738
even if you're not
a breakfast eater.

00:26:41.738 --> 00:26:43.030
This works when you travel too.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:44.655
Just get on to the
local meal schedule.

00:26:44.655 --> 00:26:47.365
Because you have a clock system
in your gut, believe it or not.

00:26:47.365 --> 00:26:49.240
You want to synchronize
that with your brain.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.910
And then activity-- getting
some sort of exercise.

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:54.220
But it takes a little bit
of work, but you can do it.

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:56.500
You can definitely do it.

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:58.330
If you're nocturnal,
that's weird,

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.658
unless it's because of
your work, in which case

00:27:00.658 --> 00:27:02.950
there are tools for shift
work that we've put out there

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:04.017
on the podcast.

00:27:04.017 --> 00:27:05.600
Your podcasts has
been a wild success.

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:07.683
How do you see it growing
over the next few years?

00:27:07.683 --> 00:27:10.938
Honestly, I try in
the tunnel of lack

00:27:10.938 --> 00:27:12.730
of understanding and
awareness about what's

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:15.110
happening with all this.

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:16.870
I really do.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:18.550
Lex suggested we do the podcast.

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:20.900
That's a true story of doing it.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:23.333
I still really do
feel very much like I

00:27:23.333 --> 00:27:24.500
did when I was a little kid.

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:26.950
I'm just going to keep
trying to learn and share.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:28.772
I'd love for people
to share the tools.

00:27:28.772 --> 00:27:29.980
I don't want credit for them.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:31.780
If people credit us, great.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:34.660
But if you think about it--
most of what we talk about

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:36.330
are not things that you buy.

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:38.830
These are tools that, again,
work the first time every time.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:41.260
I always say
behavioral tools first,

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:44.540
then nutrition, supplementation.

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:46.760
And then for some people,
prescription drugs

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:49.175
or some of these more
experimental drugs make sense.

00:27:49.175 --> 00:27:50.300
For some people it doesn't.

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:52.220
For instance, I don't think kids
should be doing psychedelics.

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.740
I mean, childhood is enough
of a psychedelic experience

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:56.720
in and of itself.

00:27:56.720 --> 00:28:01.010
But I had an amazing clinician--
he's actually a triple-board

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:03.830
certified psychiatrist
neurologist at Stanford Nolan

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:05.240
Williams on the podcast--

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:08.360
and he talked about even the
use of ibogaine and MDMA,

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.160
even in some younger
populations but again,

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:12.980
with therapeutic oversight.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:14.370
It blew my mind.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:16.852
I also didn't know this--
that MDMA I thought was toxic.

00:28:16.852 --> 00:28:18.560
Please don't just take
it off the street,

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:20.270
but if you're interested
in clinical trials,

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:22.562
there are great clinical
trials happening through maps,

00:28:22.562 --> 00:28:25.370
and you can look at
Nolan's website as well.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:27.680
Most of the knowledge
about the effects of MDMA

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:32.960
is from the LDS community
because they volunteered

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:36.560
for these studies because it's
not on the banned substance

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:38.150
list.

00:28:38.150 --> 00:28:40.160
And so there's a
lot of knowledge,

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:44.540
and they don't tend to-- or
they don't use other substances,

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:47.400
like alcohol and
marijuana and cocaine.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.850
So much of what we know
about the effects of MDMA

00:28:49.850 --> 00:28:52.520
on the body and brain
is from that community.

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.150
Other communities too as well.

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.510
So what are we going
to do with the podcast?

00:28:56.510 --> 00:29:00.560
Well, every Monday I'm going
to keep putting out episodes,

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:03.230
until they put me in that
grave with the thing.

00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:04.880
Thank you.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:05.540
Yeah.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.120
It is a labor of love,
and it's a lot of fun.

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:11.420
And we're just always trying
to make them better, clearer.

00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:13.010
Somehow they're not
getting shorter.

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.020
I always tell Rob-- this one
is going to be 90 minutes.

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:15.853
And he's like, yeah.

00:29:15.853 --> 00:29:18.260
I'll believe it when I see it.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:21.320
I think for me one
thing that has brought

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:24.050
that's really wonderful
is the opportunity

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:27.050
to learn from people
in other domains

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.517
that are far better
at putting information

00:29:29.517 --> 00:29:30.600
and things into the world.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.770
So I've been-- I hope he
doesn't mind me saying this.

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:35.160
I've been very blessed--

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.880
I've become really good
friends with Rick Rubin, who's

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:39.530
been really helpful to me.

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:41.600
He has an amazing book
on creativity coming out

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:43.190
that's not a plug for the
book, although I guess

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:44.600
I just accidentally did it.

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:47.870
But Rick of course,
is like producing.

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:50.810
Created all this amazing music.

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.440
Everything from like Run-DMC,
Beastie Boys, Slayer,

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:54.440
Johnny Cash, everything.

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:55.380
Just amazing.

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:58.220
And one of the things that
he's been impressing on me

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:02.150
is that it's very
important to stay focused

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:04.520
on the process of
what you're doing

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:10.170
and to really not get into too
much of how that's landing.

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:12.650
So I do like to hear when
things are not clear.

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:14.090
That's really helpful to me.

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:16.400
I do like to hear suggestions
about great people

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:17.420
to bring on the podcast.

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:22.830
I love criticism
most of the time.

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:26.360
I try my-- it's hard
sometimes, but I really

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:28.040
try and just absorb
it for what it is.

00:30:28.040 --> 00:30:31.190
But I love that aspect
of interacting in this,

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:36.110
even though this is very
fairly unidirectional.

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:38.550
Hopefully, there'll be more
opportunities for dialoguing

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:39.800
and learning what's out there.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.510
My real hope is that
practitioners will

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:42.900
start to incorporate things.

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:44.690
And again, it's not about me.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:48.650
I'm gleaning from fields and
discoveries of other people

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:50.420
and trying to thread
across fields.

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:53.010
So that's why I see the podcast
going just more of the same,

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:56.720
more of the same, more of
the same but a lot more

00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:59.473
and more topics as best
we can-- lots of guests.

00:30:59.473 --> 00:31:01.640
And we actually have an
episode with Rick coming out

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:03.050
at some point about
creativity, which

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:05.060
I think is one of the
more interesting aspects

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:11.150
of our being, so watch for that.

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:14.760
What is a stress inoculation
protocol for workplace anxiety

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:15.260
(speaking)?

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:17.135
Does the principle of staying
calm under high adrenaline

00:31:17.135 --> 00:31:18.540
state-- yeah, definitely.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:22.340
I think if you were to
pick some sort of practice

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:24.380
that you could do
privately and safely--

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:26.230
again, how cold should
you make the water?

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:27.980
Cold enough that it's
really uncomfortable

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:29.150
and you really,
really want to get out

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:30.530
but you can safely stay in.

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:34.020
And that's why we never say
40 degrees because you can't

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:35.270
kill yourself with cold water.

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:37.860
It's just hard to do.

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:40.910
You have to get really, really
cold before you kill yourself.

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:43.040
And open bodies of
water aren't good.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.720
Actually, I told my
friend Samer Hattar.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:46.637
He's the director of
chronobiology unit

00:31:46.637 --> 00:31:48.470
at the National Institutes
of mental health.

00:31:48.470 --> 00:31:49.722
He came on the podcast.

00:31:49.722 --> 00:31:51.680
Got him really-- he's
great about all the stuff

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:52.665
on light and sleep.

00:31:52.665 --> 00:31:54.290
Taught me a lot of
that over the years.

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:54.860
We're good friends.

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:56.443
I told him about the
cold water thing,

00:31:56.443 --> 00:32:00.230
and he got into some river in
Bethesda and almost drowned.

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:02.480
And Sam right now-- he has
a story about how he almost

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:04.880
drowned, and what he was--
and he thought about a paper

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:08.750
he wanted to write while
he was almost drowning.

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:11.990
So just be careful-- open bodies
of water, that kind of thing.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:16.280
But I think you can quickly
see within about a week or so

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:19.680
of doing some sort of
deliberate adrenaline release.

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:21.200
It could be cyclic
hyperventilation,

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.780
25 hyperventilated breaths
with a short breath hold,

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:25.550
repeat, done two or three times.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:27.830
We have good data to
support that in the lab.

00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:30.718
You see massive shifts in
people's baseline level.

00:32:30.718 --> 00:32:32.510
You become a little
bit more like Costello.

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:33.530
You really do.

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:37.640
And the way to think about it
is, if you are more on a seesaw

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:42.230
then on the continuum, you get
better at loosening that hinge

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:43.740
and controlling that hinge.

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:47.340
Or you can imagine moving
up and down that seesaw

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:48.753
a little bit more easily.

00:32:48.753 --> 00:32:50.670
And then of course, you
have to place yourself

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:51.783
into the environment.

00:32:51.783 --> 00:32:53.700
You have to test yourself
in that environment,

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:55.658
and some people will do
Toastmasters and things

00:32:55.658 --> 00:32:57.460
like that, and it can work.

00:32:57.460 --> 00:33:00.322
My way-- I don't know if
it works for everybody.

00:33:00.322 --> 00:33:01.530
My way is just start talking.

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:02.930
Don't stop.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:03.930
I can't feel the stress.

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:08.250
What do you think will
be the next hot topic/new

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:09.840
trend in the field of
neuroscience behavioral

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:10.500
therapeutics?

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:15.460
Ooh, I like that because I have
a lot of opinions about that.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.430
First of all, I do
not think it's going

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:19.155
to be brain machine interface.

00:33:19.155 --> 00:33:21.780
My good friend, Eddie Chang, who
I've known since we were nine,

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:22.738
he came on the podcast.

00:33:22.738 --> 00:33:23.700
He works on epilepsy.

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:25.170
He's a chair of neurosurgery.

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:30.510
We had a bird club when we
were kids at two members.

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:34.518
You had to know the names
of all the talking birds,

00:33:34.518 --> 00:33:36.810
and then you had to know
which one was the best talker,

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:38.780
and it's the minor bird.

00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:40.907
And of course, no
one wanted to join.

00:33:40.907 --> 00:33:41.990
No one even took the test.

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:45.800
But he became a neurosurgeon and
does brain machine interface.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:47.930
He's doing truly incredible
work getting people

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:51.320
with locked in syndrome to
speak through a device implanted

00:33:51.320 --> 00:33:52.250
under the skull.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:54.170
They just think what
they want to say.

00:33:54.170 --> 00:33:56.240
These are people who
haven't moved or shared

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:59.000
a word with anybody, and
they're now communicating,

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.010
but here's what's
really cool and--

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:04.230
he has also realized that
facial expression is a lot.

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:06.290
It's one thing to
see on a screen what

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:09.560
somebody in a chair or a
hospital bed is thinking.

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:12.413
That's wonderful,
but facial expression

00:34:12.413 --> 00:34:13.580
is such a rich part of this.

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:15.630
So this is a really
good use of AI.

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:19.012
He's now created very
realistic iPad images

00:34:19.012 --> 00:34:20.929
of that person's face,
and so they're actually

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:22.070
speaking the words.

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.050
And people form a
deep relationship

00:34:24.050 --> 00:34:27.530
to the person who's right
next to their avatar,

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:30.650
so that's a positive
use, I think, of avatars,

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:32.120
and Eddie is doing amazing work.

00:34:32.120 --> 00:34:35.780
That kind of work-- a brain
machine interface neural link,

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:39.650
et cetera, I think is going
to be very useful and popular

00:34:39.650 --> 00:34:42.770
in the realm of therapeutics
for Parkinson's, movement

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:46.280
disorders, epilepsy,
locked-in syndrome, et cetera.

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:50.929
I think we are many, many, many
decades from chip implantation

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:53.840
into the brain for things
like enhancing memory,

00:34:53.840 --> 00:34:55.998
and frankly I wouldn't want it.

00:34:55.998 --> 00:34:58.040
Not because I wouldn't
want to enhance my memory,

00:34:58.040 --> 00:34:59.555
but because of what
I said earlier.

00:34:59.555 --> 00:35:02.180
Is that the nervous system has
a certain amount of real estate,

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:05.870
and you don't want to make
that real estate very lopsided.

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:08.510
And so I think we're going to
see something very different

00:35:08.510 --> 00:35:13.140
in the next 10, 20
years, and I hope, hope,

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:16.070
hope this carries over
to younger populations.

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:17.690
I think we're going
to hopefully start

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:20.330
learning about our nervous
system and what it can do.

00:35:20.330 --> 00:35:24.170
And the fact that we have these
pre-existing circuits in us

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:25.940
that we can learn
to leverage that

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:27.330
work first time every time.

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:29.300
So I'm strongly
biased in my answer,

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:33.410
but I think that it still
remains an open question,

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:36.410
for instance, whether or not
people could require less

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:39.470
perhaps or no medication
for certain things.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:41.720
And I say certain things
because for conditions

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:45.320
like schizophrenia, bipolar
in particular medication,

00:35:45.320 --> 00:35:46.580
OCD has proved very effective.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:48.080
But then there are
a whole other set

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.660
of conditions like
depression and anxiety

00:35:50.660 --> 00:35:52.400
for which behavioral
tools really work,

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:55.170
but most people just don't
even know they exist.

00:35:55.170 --> 00:36:00.380
So that's my hope, and again,
the therapeutic community

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:02.413
could try and expand
their toolkit.

00:36:02.413 --> 00:36:03.830
I also think we're
starting to see

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:06.455
a blurring of the lines between
different fields of psychology,

00:36:06.455 --> 00:36:09.050
so it's no longer
psychoanalytic versus

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:10.760
cognitive behavioral
versus dialectic

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:12.733
versus EMDR versus hypnosis.

00:36:12.733 --> 00:36:14.150
That it's all going
to be governed

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:15.770
by some central principles.

00:36:15.770 --> 00:36:19.070
All those camps-- it's
just silly frankly to me.

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:21.530
It makes sense academically
why those came to be,

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:23.300
but in neuroscience
we had the same thing.

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:24.425
You used to have to pick.

00:36:24.425 --> 00:36:26.300
It was like a John Hughes
film from the '80s.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:29.892
Are you going to be a jock
or a punker or a popular kid.

00:36:29.892 --> 00:36:31.850
Now, it's not like that,
and half this audience

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:33.290
is looking at me like,
what are you talking about?

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:34.732
And that's exactly the point.

00:36:34.732 --> 00:36:36.440
Which is that I'll
never forget I grew up

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:37.190
in the skateboard thing.

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:39.080
The first time I saw someone
wearing skateboarding shoes,

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:39.860
I was like, damn.

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:40.430
They skateboard.

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:41.305
And they're like, no.

00:36:41.305 --> 00:36:44.060
They just-- people are
just wearing this stuff.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:47.550
So well, you start to realize
it's all blended together,

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:48.500
which is great.

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:49.888
And in the field
of neuroscience,

00:36:49.888 --> 00:36:50.930
you used to have to pick.

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:53.420
Are you an anatomist
or a physiologist?

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:55.100
Are you into neural computation?

00:36:55.100 --> 00:36:57.800
Now, your lab has to do it all,
or you collaborate with people.

00:36:57.800 --> 00:36:59.390
Those divisions
have really melted

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:01.220
because people are
interested in questions,

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:02.960
and they're
interested in answers.

00:37:02.960 --> 00:37:06.080
And don't get me started,
but the careerism

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:08.060
of the requirement
for everyone to have

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:10.430
their own independent
laboratory and say this

00:37:10.430 --> 00:37:14.400
is my mission, that is one of
the worst things about science.

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:18.590
Because everything we know
says that collaboration

00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:22.220
collaboration, collaboration,
leads to faster progress.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:24.950
And I'm not going to take on
the whole academic system.

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:27.140
It's been very
good to me, but I'd

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:29.480
like to see a blurring
of the boundaries.

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.310
It used to be that
labs weren't-- here,

00:37:31.310 --> 00:37:32.960
I'm really guilty, Huberman lab.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.840
But it used to be that labs were
named after the problem they

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:36.740
worked on.

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:40.250
Vision Lab, Stress
Lab, Happiness Lab.

00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:42.117
I like that quite
a bit more, but I

00:37:42.117 --> 00:37:44.450
screwed up and called this
whole thing the Huberman Lab.

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:46.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:48.013 --> 00:37:49.930
You have had a number
of performance interests

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:50.980
and fitness experts
on your show.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:53.147
What changes have you made
to your fitness protocol,

00:37:53.147 --> 00:37:54.070
including nutrition?

00:37:54.070 --> 00:37:56.560
Oh.

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:57.580
Yeah.

00:37:57.580 --> 00:38:00.790
Well, I'm an omnivore,
so I like those things

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:04.900
they call carbohydrates, and
I eat them in moderation.

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:08.770
And so I've never really been
too extreme about any of this,

00:38:08.770 --> 00:38:11.020
or the fasting thing,
although I do just--

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:12.490
I'm not very hungry
in the morning.

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:16.040
That's also because I like
to eat a lot at night.

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:17.830
I wake up not that hungry.

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:19.270
So what have I changed?

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:20.770
Well, in terms of
the fitness stuff,

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:23.050
I've definitely
started to incorporate

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:26.620
more nasal breathing when I do
cardiovascular work because it

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:30.100
has eliminated any
sleep apnea I had.

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:32.500
And sleep apnea is
very, very bad--

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:34.930
very, very bad.

00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:37.090
I wish I could say
snoring was no big deal,

00:38:37.090 --> 00:38:41.020
but we know based on work at the
Stanford Sleep Lab, Penn Sleep

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:43.960
Lab, other sleep labs that
people who have sleep apnea

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:47.600
are really in for trouble
for a number of reasons.

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:49.450
So you want to learn
to be a nasal breather,

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:51.010
and if-- some people
will tape their mouth

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:52.940
shut with medical tape
when they go to sleep.

00:38:52.940 --> 00:38:55.780
Other people will just start
doing cardiovascular work,

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:57.130
keeping their mouth closed.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:59.560
And that requires that
you not go too intensely,

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:04.060
but it does create a dilation
of the nasal passages.

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:05.230
Those sinuses can dilate.

00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:06.500
So I've definitely done that.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:08.260
And at the end of
training, I try and do

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:10.060
a one-minute or
three-minute decompress.

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:12.190
Not immediately look
at my phone to learn

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:15.460
to shift from high
intensity thinking

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:18.795
and to lower intensity thinking
and shift throughout the day.

00:39:18.795 --> 00:39:20.170
Task switching,
I think, is going

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:25.090
to be a big area of science
and neuroscience in general.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:27.520
We still don't know
how to task switch

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:30.190
well-- how to shift the
mind from focus to defocus

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.210
and back again.

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:32.830
That's something
that-- by the way,

00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:36.160
this is, I think a rich
opportunity for people

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:36.908
to develop tools.

00:39:36.908 --> 00:39:38.200
We don't know a lot about that.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.210
We got too caught up
on consciousness flow

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:41.560
and free will.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:44.980
And that stuff is great, but
as you can tell, I like tools.

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:46.390
I like physiology.

00:39:46.390 --> 00:39:48.380
I like the things that
work in my lifetime

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:50.800
and that we can figure out
and agree upon in my lifetime.

00:39:53.410 --> 00:39:55.850
Let's see.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:56.480
Got it.

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:58.780
And I'm told that
this final question--

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:00.613
when they put the clock
up here earlier too,

00:40:00.613 --> 00:40:02.572
they said we're going to
run it for 60 minutes.

00:40:02.572 --> 00:40:04.690
My first question was,
where's the snooze button?

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:06.370
So I'm going to keep
going, but I think

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:07.240
they're going to hold me to it.

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:09.198
And I know, people, this
is New York after all.

00:40:09.198 --> 00:40:10.900
There are other
fun things to do.

00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:12.670
For things that take
a long time-- career,

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:14.410
pursuing a degree--

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:17.890
is there a right way to know
that we're on the right path?

00:40:17.890 --> 00:40:20.170
Is there a way to know
we're on the right path?

00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:21.940
Thank you for that question.

00:40:21.940 --> 00:40:27.920
I get asked this a
lot, and gosh, there's

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:30.440
so much information out there.

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:32.720
So much information about this.

00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:37.610
We don't yet have a
gauge of whether or not

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.950
we're in too much
stress or not, but there

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:43.490
is one tool that's used in
the free diving community

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:44.960
and in other
communities that you

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:48.715
can use as a thermometer of
how well you're functioning.

00:40:48.715 --> 00:40:50.840
And some of you may know
it already, and if you do,

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:51.340
forgive me.

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:54.300
It's this carbon
dioxide tolerance test.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.510
So why am I answering
this question this way?

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.060
Well, I like to start
with actionable tools.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:02.360
If, for instance, you were to
just take three or four breaths

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.970
and then take a big
deep breath and then

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:07.098
do a very slow
controlled exhale--

00:41:07.098 --> 00:41:09.140
could be through your nose
or through your mouth.

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:10.610
Ideally, through your nose.

00:41:10.610 --> 00:41:13.100
And you're trying to make that
exhale as long as possible,

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:15.380
until your lungs are
empty, and you time that.

00:41:15.380 --> 00:41:18.680
That's called the carbon
dioxide discard rate

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:19.910
or the exhale discard rate.

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:24.260
And it tells you how well you're
controlling your diaphragm

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:26.330
using something called
the phrenic nerve.

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:30.350
It also tells you how well
you're managing carbon dioxide

00:41:30.350 --> 00:41:32.240
and how well you're
managing stress.

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:34.288
And if you're very
stressed, that number

00:41:34.288 --> 00:41:35.330
will be very, very short.

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:36.260
And I'm not talking
about how long

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:37.468
you can sit with lungs empty.

00:41:37.468 --> 00:41:41.560
I'm talking about an honest
appraisal of how long

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:43.300
you can control that exhale for.

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:45.970
And if it's anywhere
from 0 to 20 seconds,

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:48.700
your stress level is high.

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:52.600
And if it's from 20 to 40
seconds, it's moderate.

00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:54.400
And longer than 40
seconds means you

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:59.710
have good control over
your carbon dioxide system,

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:00.460
more or less.

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:02.200
Now, these are averages,
and guess what?

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:05.140
Has nothing to do with
fitness, and also, it

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:08.455
has nothing to do with you per
se because if you do this when

00:42:08.455 --> 00:42:10.330
you first wake up after
a good night's sleep,

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:11.770
you'll have a long discard rate.

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:13.930
If you do this after
running, you'll

00:42:13.930 --> 00:42:15.370
have a short discard rate.

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:16.660
You're not out of breath.

00:42:16.660 --> 00:42:18.730
You're just not managing
the system very well.

00:42:18.730 --> 00:42:21.730
So you can touch into this
every once in a while as a blood

00:42:21.730 --> 00:42:23.510
pressure reading type thing.

00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:25.820
This is very back of the
envelope it's not perfect,

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:28.840
but it works well enough
that alongside things

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:33.400
like resting heart rate, heart
rate variability, et cetera,

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:36.460
you can get a window into how
well you're managing stress.

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:38.230
Why am I answering
this question this way?

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:40.235
Well, this is
something I recommend

00:42:40.235 --> 00:42:41.860
doing every once in
a while, especially

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:45.970
if you are in a period of a
career or any kind of pursuit

00:42:45.970 --> 00:42:49.210
where you are feeling
like you're grinding.

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:54.370
I think once your carbon dioxide
discard rate starts to really

00:42:54.370 --> 00:42:56.980
get shorter and shorter,
you're having trouble sleeping,

00:42:56.980 --> 00:43:01.390
I think it's time to focus on
re-establishing that buoyancy

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:04.117
to your nervous system
because then and only then can

00:43:04.117 --> 00:43:05.950
you make good judgments
about whether or not

00:43:05.950 --> 00:43:08.290
you're in the right
trajectory for you.

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:10.360
Now, in terms of the larger
psychological themes,

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:12.652
are you doing something that
brings you meaning or not?

00:43:12.652 --> 00:43:14.560
That gets into some
complicated territory.

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:17.197
We're very good at assigning
meaning retrospectively,

00:43:17.197 --> 00:43:19.030
and saying, well, that
was a good experience

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:21.250
because we had it, and we
learned from it, et cetera.

00:43:21.250 --> 00:43:23.500
But I think most people
would like to avoid things

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:26.320
that they can only
look back on and say,

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:28.850
it was useful because I
learned something from it.

00:43:28.850 --> 00:43:32.740
And for that, I'll
just give the default,

00:43:32.740 --> 00:43:35.000
but I think, at least
to me, accurate answer,

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:37.150
which is the more
often that you can

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:41.910
tap into that feeling of
excitement and delight

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:44.430
in your work, even
if from small things

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:47.400
or from surprising things, or
from the social interactions

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:49.830
that you're able to
glean from that work,

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:54.240
the longer and better you're
going to be able to pursue

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:54.990
that line of work.

00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:57.610
For me is in graduate
school, I was very isolated.

00:43:57.610 --> 00:43:58.800
I worked alone in the lab.

00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:00.300
Maybe it was because
of the tin foil

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:02.280
I put on the walls
or the windows.

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:03.060
I don't know.

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.070
In fact, my graduate
advisor's one complaint

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:06.937
was that I seemed unfriendly.

00:44:06.937 --> 00:44:07.770
I wasn't unfriendly.

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:09.997
I was just busy, but I got
to be very good friends

00:44:09.997 --> 00:44:11.580
with the janitorial
staff because they

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:14.160
were the only one around
at the time I was working--

00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:15.390
were a few other people.

00:44:15.390 --> 00:44:17.580
And those small
interactions actually became

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:22.160
very significant to me and
became sources of brief--

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:23.630
but to me, at
least, at the time,

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:25.162
meaningful social exchange.

00:44:25.162 --> 00:44:26.870
And of course,
eventually, I made friends

00:44:26.870 --> 00:44:29.930
and had relationships of
other kinds and things

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:32.400
that were healthy as well.

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:36.800
But I think learning to tap into
this love of what you're doing

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:39.120
is sometimes hard, but
you have to look for it.

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:41.120
It's an active process,
and I'll default

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:45.197
to the work and the podcast
that's coming with Rick Rubin.

00:44:45.197 --> 00:44:47.780
And he is a lot of what he talks
about in terms of creativity.

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:50.420
Is about accessing
what he and others have

00:44:50.420 --> 00:44:52.220
talked about as the source.

00:44:52.220 --> 00:44:54.240
If that is an abstract,
I don't know what is.

00:44:54.240 --> 00:44:57.890
But the source is this
ability to see yourself

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:00.830
as more of a portal for
getting certain things done

00:45:00.830 --> 00:45:04.100
in the world than
being so careerist

00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:06.440
and focused on whether or
not outcomes are really

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:07.970
matching what you need.

00:45:07.970 --> 00:45:10.250
It involves some
mental flexibility,

00:45:10.250 --> 00:45:13.200
and of course, it's OK
to pivot back and forth.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:16.400
But we can only
access this feeling

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:18.050
of delight and joy
and this feeling

00:45:18.050 --> 00:45:21.590
that we're somehow connected to
some larger theme, aka meaning,

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:25.100
I think, when we are able to be
calm enough and not so focused.

00:45:25.100 --> 00:45:26.550
But in order to
get anything done,

00:45:26.550 --> 00:45:28.717
we have to be hyper focused,
and that brings me back

00:45:28.717 --> 00:45:31.430
to the basic principle of
today's whole discussion, which

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:33.800
is that it's not
about landing yourself

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.900
in a state of focus
motivation and drive

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.400
or in a state of deep sleep.

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:38.720
Certainly, not in a coma.

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:42.950
It's about being able to move up
and down the various continuums

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:46.970
that allow you to access
focus and real gas

00:45:46.970 --> 00:45:50.780
pedal down to the floor
kind of thinking and action

00:45:50.780 --> 00:45:53.930
but then also deliberately
back off transition

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:55.280
to periods of rest.

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:57.975
And the real key is for
you to feel like you're

00:45:57.975 --> 00:45:58.850
in the driver's seat.

00:45:58.850 --> 00:46:02.000
If we know anything from
the last 100-plus years

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:04.850
of psychology and
neuroscience literature,

00:46:04.850 --> 00:46:07.400
it's that if an
animal or a person

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:10.910
feels that they are in control
of the physiological process

00:46:10.910 --> 00:46:13.520
within them, and they know
they can get themselves

00:46:13.520 --> 00:46:16.970
out, some way somehow at some
point and back into a state

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:19.310
that they want somehow,
some way, at some point,

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:24.290
well, then, all the language
around meaning and happiness

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:26.220
and delight starts to emerge.

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:29.750
So learn to move along
those continuums.

00:46:29.750 --> 00:46:32.510
Learn to do it
deliberately, and I wish you

00:46:32.510 --> 00:46:33.680
the very best of luck in it.

00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:34.550
I know it works.

00:46:34.550 --> 00:46:36.174
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:36.174 --> 00:46:37.650
Thank you.

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:39.126
Thanks so much.

00:46:39.126 --> 00:46:40.110
Thank you.

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:42.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:43.540
Thank you.

00:46:43.540 --> 00:46:44.910
Thanks so much for coming out.

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:46.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:46.210 --> 00:46:51.120
I really appreciate
it and grateful

00:46:51.120 --> 00:46:52.470
to our sponsors, of course.

00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:54.580
Thank you and to all
of you for your time.

00:46:54.580 --> 00:46:56.640
I know there's a lot
to do in the city,

00:46:56.640 --> 00:46:58.560
and I hope everyone enjoys it.

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:01.260
And of course, I'd be
remiss if I didn't say,

00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:03.000
thank you for your
interest in science.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:05.100
[CHEERS]

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:08.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

